# Global ignores, wired via core.excludesfile in .gitconfig.

# herdr-handoff reply drops. A handoff asks the receiving agent to write its
# answer into its OWN working directory, because that is the one place every
# harness writes without a sandbox prompt -- which means these land inside
# whatever repo that agent is working in. The sender normally collects and
# deletes them, but it cannot if it gave up first (the recipient stalled on a
# permission dialog, say), and one such orphan reached a commit before this
# line existed. Ignoring them globally makes that unrepeatable.
.herdr-reply-*.md
